ometimes like anxiety that  there are things that are being picked  apart to such a degree yeah it's uh some  some of the things are of course like  our mistake because as I said we are not  the experts right and some of these  weapons of course we live in at least I  live in Europe it's not that easy to get  your hands on any of this like if we  were a U.S Studio it would be related to  just like go to whatever pawn shop get  like a bit early or something and and  then play with it right but we don't  really have the chance to to get  especially this really rare historical  weapons sometimes we we make a mistake  because we really don't know how it  actually worked sometimes it happens  that the limitations of a video game can  and the constrain us and we we cannot do  it perfectly realistic because of  whatever it could be animation  constraints could be maybe it's too  expensive or too many parts or something  for it for it to run we try very hard to  mimic real life sometimes we animate  stuff we try to make it as close to how  it should move in the real world like  timing wise how slow it should be how  heavy it should be but sometimes because  of the constraints and um there are so  many different weapons in game that we  have to balance them obviously like you  said you're in Europe and Singapore you  don't have access to a lot of these  weapons so what resources do you use  when when it comes to you know creating  virtual representations these weapons  like uh before  um we create uh something new a new  weapon for example  um weapon art for example Alex uh he  will source for all the blueprints that  he can find the references and he will  show us  how the gun mechanisms work and what are  the things to take note of and stuff is  very essential for us to look at  whatever blueprints or references that  we can find because Hunt is very  realistic we want to make it as  authentic as possible from the from the  art side I would say definitely yeah  blueprint or a lifesaver if we can get  our hands on them because with those we  make sure that the the proportions of  the weapon are correct not only because  like pictures can be maybe a little bit  distorted or skewed or something  depending on how how rare the weapon is  there might not even be a lot of  material online to to find information  about it right not blueprints or manuals  or something so sometimes you have to  kind of figure it out by yourself a  really high definition pictures are also  great that those allow us to basically  go really deep and put all the markings  and all the the text and all the little  like stamps and details on the weapons  like to really nail them and another  another great resource or video so  there's like some historical  um YouTube channels that help us a lot  that they they take these 19th century  weapons and like really analyze them  their history their design their their  patterns different patterns uh  throughout the their their lifetime and  uh yeah basically seeing seeing it in  movement seeing the the people handle it  like how it you know opening it  reloading it gives a real sense of you  know how difficult it is to to operate  yeah we need to get a lot of references  um since yeah we we of course cannot get  them so  the sparks llr in particular I believe  you were the first  um Studio at least publicly to come out  and say hey like this note was made in a  video from from Gamespot and we've  changed it what what made you want to  want to do that like there's there's  other weapons that were critiqued but  with the with the sparks in particular  what made you sort of take note look at  you know what was said and go back and  be like yeah we are going to change this  reload so for this box that hammer the  extra action on the hammer is we have  seen it is really a extra action and  because we want to uh animate as  realistically as possible so the  Redundant action should be taken out I  think we did not change the actual  reload time even if it doesn't do the  extraction with the hammer the reload  time stays the same and this this is for  balancing reasons because if we did it  more realistic then of course the reload  will be order and it would make the  Sparks more powerful in relation to  other guns and yeah so sometimes we make  reloading times uh longer or shorter for  for balancing the weapon and as we said  before we have so many weapons at this  point that we need to really take this  more variable uh constraints or more  variable things like the reload time  like maybe the damage or something as as  everything as we can to balance the  weapon in a but it still it feels  realistic so it was the sort of like a  meeting or you know a slack conversation  being like hey this has been pointed out  it's probably something that we can do  how how did how did that go down I'm not  sure exactly who pointed it out because  again we're really passionate about your  your Express react Series so a lot of a  lot of people in the team uh watch it  and uh and yeah I I guess it must have  been in animation because it wasn't  because of course that doesn't bend on  the art so it wasn't on us but some  somebody pointed it out in in the chat  and they were like well yeah let's  change it you know this is wrong and we  got this valuable bit of feedback so  let's apply it  thank you  your studio is very tied to your  community and engage with the community  a lot was there you know when you when  you posted that patch saying hey we've  updated the Sparks and this is why were  there was there like any interesting  responses from the community was it like  like hey cool like you took took that on  board or other people absolutely that  wasn't necessary yeah what was the what  was the response yeah the community was  really excited uh because uh the people  who are passionate about hand they're  usually also passionate about these  historical weapons and the realism and  seeing that we are that we are also  those people we are also part of the the  community that is uh very interested in  history very interested in in this like  all firearms and and they were really  excited that we actually listened and  that we took took notice and uh and did  the changes they were really excited it  was really nice to see it brings kind of  the the development team and the  community together in a way right  because we are basically fans of the  same things we we are we are them  basically like I was a member of the  community way way before I joined the  dev team off the back of of you know  either the the video itself or just for  the research and reading into things  have there been any other weapons that  you've sort of gone back and taken a  second look at and been like we could we  could tweak this tie it into reality and  it's real world counterparts a little  more yes there there was one one more  thing Jonathan noticed in the first  video that the some of our revolvers  like the the conversion and the packs do  not use the ejector rods to to reload  instead they do like some kind of  rubbing and then the the weapon uh  cartridges come out this is for a reason  but it's a somewhat kind of uh technical  obscure one basically our weapons are  modular which means that they are  comprised of of different parts right so  it's usually the the barrel the receiver  and the stock and normally to to kind of  make it easier on the on the animation  team and the technical art team only the  the receiver is animated so basically  only the the drum and the the trigger  the hammer basically the things that  usually move when the weapon Cycles uh  only that part is animated the problem  with those revolvers is that the the  ejector Rod is in the barrel so we  either make like a kind of really big  receiver with like a lot of parts and  stuff or we still need to split it  because we need to make for example  longer pistol versions or like local  socks or whatever so in this case i